-- title:
--   The commutators of a generating set with a set generating one lower central factor generate the next factor
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a group with lower central series $G = \Gamma_0 \supseteq \Gamma_1
--   \supseteq \cdots$, where $\Gamma_{k+1}$ is the commutator subgroup of $\Gamma_k$ with $G$. Let
--   $X$ generate $G$, and let $Y$ be a subset of $\Gamma_k$ whose image generates
--   $\Gamma_k/\Gamma_{k+1}$ -- precisely, $\Gamma_k$ is contained in the subgroup generated by $Y$
--   together with $\Gamma_{k+1}$. Then $\Gamma_{k+1}$ is contained in the subgroup generated by the
--   commutators $xyx^{-1}y^{-1}$, for $x \in X$ and $y \in Y$, together with $\Gamma_{k+2}$; that
--   is, those commutators generate $\Gamma_{k+1}/\Gamma_{k+2}$.
--
--   The mechanism is that $\Gamma_{k+1}/\Gamma_{k+2}$ is central in $G/\Gamma_{k+2}$, so the
--   commutator is bilinear modulo $\Gamma_{k+2}$ and it suffices to check the generators. The
--   commutator is written out as a product rather than with bracket notation, and is $xyx^{-1}y^{-1}$
--   in that order.
--
--   Three points of precision. The indexing starts at zero, $\Gamma_0 = G$ and $\Gamma_1 = [G,G]$;
--   authors who index from one call this subgroup $\gamma_{k+2}$. The family of commutators is
--   asymmetric -- only $xyx^{-1}y^{-1}$ with $x \in X$ and $y \in Y$, not the other order and not
--   commutators within $X$ or within $Y$. And the conclusion is stated as one containment, that
--   $\Gamma_{k+1}$ lies in the join of the generated subgroup with $\Gamma_{k+2}$; the reverse holds
--   automatically, since each such commutator already lies in $\Gamma_{k+1}$, so the two sides are in
--   fact equal and the commutators really do generate the factor.
--
--   Nothing here is vacuous -- taking $X = G$ and $Y = \Gamma_k$ satisfies the hypotheses for every
--   $G$ and $k$ -- but two degenerate cases are permitted. At $k = 0$ the hypothesis on $Y$ is
--   automatic, and $Y$ may be empty, in which case the hypotheses force
--   $\Gamma_k = \Gamma_{k+1}$ and the conclusion reduces to the true statement
--   $\Gamma_{k+1} = \Gamma_{k+2}$.
-- source:
--   Proved in the course of the Wolf mission (J. A. Wolf, Growth of finitely generated solvable groups and curvature of Riemannian manifolds, Journal of Differential Geometry 2 (1968) 421-446, https://doi.org/10.4310/jdg/1214428658); this is the half of Wolf's Lemma 3.7 (p. 428) that his argument establishes; the remaining half of that lemma, the choice of an independent generating subfamily, does not follow from finite generation alone and is not claimed here.

import Mathlib

namespace LowerCentralSeries

theorem succ_le_closure_commutatorElements {G : Type*} [Group G] (k : ℕ) (X Y : Set G)
    (hX : Subgroup.closure X = ⊤)
    (hY : Y ⊆ ((⊤ : Subgroup G).lowerCentralSeries k : Set G))
    (hYgen : (⊤ : Subgroup G).lowerCentralSeries k ≤
      Subgroup.closure Y ⊔ (⊤ : Subgroup G).lowerCentralSeries (k + 1)) :
    (⊤ : Subgroup G).lowerCentralSeries (k + 1)
      ≤ Subgroup.closure {g : G | ∃ x ∈ X, ∃ y ∈ Y, x * y * x⁻¹ * y⁻¹ = g}
          ⊔ (⊤ : Subgroup G).lowerCentralSeries (k + 2) := by
  sorry

end LowerCentralSeries
